 ## 4. Token plan
 
-Write the plan in this shape. The values are yours to choose; there are deliberately no example values here, so nothing anchors them.
+Start with the **source sheet**. Naming an object is not enough: unless its visible properties are given to the parts the page needs anyway, the build falls back to stock parts and the object survives only in one component.
+
+```
+Source sheet:
+  object   <one real thing, named exactly> — why it does this subject's job
+  usual    <how pages of this kind usually look: ground, type, the main control or data mark, surface>
+  borrow   4–6 visible properties, each given to a part the page already needs, with that part's form at 320:
+             its lettering → the display face, and where it appears | 320: <its form>
+             its marks and their states (a flag raised or lowered, a hole punched or not) → the main control or data mark in each state | 320: <its form>
+             its frame, ruling, or material → one part of the page frame (header, dividers, table ruling, ground) | 320: <its form>
+             one habit (how it counts, how it marks an entry, how it shows a correction) → one element the brief already asks for | 320: <its form>
+  departs  from `usual` on two or more of: ground value, display genre, the look of the main control or data mark, surface
+  words    the object's own nouns that the product does not use, listed; they never appear in labels, help, or messages
+```
+
+- **Properties, not pictures.** Borrow what the object does visually. Never draw the object, add a part to display it, or spread its texture as decoration; a part the page does not need is not added because the object has one.
+- **Controls stay controls.** The main control stays its native element (button, checkbox, radio, input, select), restyled after the object, with its state, if it has one, in visible text, a 3:1 boundary, and visible focus.
+- **A mark keeps one meaning and the hierarchy.** A borrowed mark stands for one state of the product, and nothing else. Its full-strength form goes on what the user must look at or act on; repeated and finished items get a quieter form.
+- **The words stay the product's.** The object shapes the look; labels and messages name what the user controls ("On", "Save", "Share"), never the object's parts.
+- **The same part at 320.** A borrowed part's 320 form is the same part made narrower, not a different one: its mark keeps its shape, meaning, and word, and any legend or label stays beside what it explains. A part that is a table stays a table at 768 while its columns fit (fold a minor column under the first, or move a sidebar above the content, before reflowing); at 320 each row becomes one short block, its name and the figure or mark acted on first, the other cells labelled side by side rather than one per line. If a property cannot hold at 320, give it to another part instead of changing it there.
+
+Then write the plan in this shape. The values are yours to choose; there are deliberately no example values here, so nothing anchors them.
 
